catalog<pre><code>from anvil.api.items.crafting.catalog import CraftingItemCatalog

catalog = CraftingItemCatalog()
catalog.add_group(ItemCategory.Items, "Ember Materials", dust, [dust, crystal])
catalog.queue()
</code></pre> <pre><code>"minecraft:crafting_items_catalog": {
  "categories": [
    {
      "category_name": "items",
      "groups": [{"group_identifier": {"icon": "tut:ember_dust", "name": "tag.tut:ember_materials"}, "items": ["tut:ember_dust", "tut:ember_crystal"]}]
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>add_group(category, name, icon, items)</code> creates a named group; the name becomes a localized <code>tag.&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code> key.</li> <li><code>add_loose_items(category, items)</code> adds items to a tab without a group.</li> <li><code>CraftingItemCatalog()</code> is a single shared file, so groups can be added from several modules.</li> </ul> <p>Menu category versus catalog</p> <p><code>set_menu_category(ItemCategory.Items, ItemGroups.Copper)</code> on the item or block description puts it in a vanilla group. The catalog is for your own groups. You can also pass the catalog's <code>tag.&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code> string as the group of <code>set_menu_category</code>.</p>

loot table<pre><code>from anvil.api.actors.components import EntityLoot
from anvil.api.vanilla.items import MinecraftItemTypes
from anvil.api.world.loot_tables import LootTable


def ember_moth_loot():
    table = LootTable("ember_moth")
    pool = table.pool(rolls=1)
    pool.entry("tut:ember_dust", count=1, weight=4)
    pool.entry(MinecraftItemTypes.Gunpowder(), count=1, weight=1)
    pool.entry(None, weight=2)

    rare = table.pool(rolls=1)
    rare.conditions.killed_by_player().random_chance_with_looting(0.05, 0.02)
    rare.entry(MinecraftItemTypes.BlazePowder(), count=1)
    table.queue()
    return table


server.components.add(EntityLoot(table=ember_moth_loot()))
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 1,
      "entries": [
        {"type": "item", "name": "tut:ember_dust", "count": 1, "weight": 4},
        {"type": "item", "name": "minecraft:gunpowder", "count": 1, "weight": 1},
        {"type": "empty", "count": 1, "weight": 2}
      ]
    },
    {
      "rolls": 1,
      "conditions": [
        {"condition": "killed_by_player"},
        {"condition": "random_chance_with_looting", "chance": 0.05, "looting_multiplier": 0.02}
      ],
      "entries": [{"type": "item", "name": "minecraft:blaze_powder", "count": 1, "weight": 1}]
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>table.pool(rolls)</code> adds a pool; <code>rolls</code> can be a <code>(min, max)</code> tuple.</li> <li><code>pool.entry(item, count, weight)</code> takes an item, a block, a string identifier, another <code>LootTable</code> or <code>None</code> for an empty entry.</li> <li><code>pool.conditions</code> has the loot conditions (<code>killed_by_player</code>, <code>random_chance</code>, <code>match_tool</code>, ...).</li> <li><code>entry.functions</code> has the loot functions: <code>entry.functions.SetCount((2, 5))</code>, <code>.EnchantWithLevels((20, 30), treasure=True)</code>, <code>.SetName("...")</code> and so on.</li> <li>Blocks use the same tables: <code>BlockLootTable(value=table)</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Where the file goes</p> <p>The table exports to <code>loot_tables/&lt;namespace&gt;/ember_moth.loot_table.json</code>. <code>EntityLoot(table=...)</code> accepts the <code>LootTable</code> object or a path string.</p>
